
  One consequence common to all three: [38;5;153mtrack()[39m is async, so [38;5;153mlogin()[39m becomes async, so the submit handler at [38;5;153mapp.js:17[39m
  becomes async too. That ripple is unavoidable once you chose an async seam, and it's the right trade — it's what keeps
  a future network transport from being an interface migration.

  A second consequence worth surfacing now: [38;5;153mlogin()[39m currently lives in [38;5;153mapp.js[39m alongside DOM code, which makes it
  untestable without a DOM. Every approach below moves it to [38;5;153msrc/auth.js[39m so it can be imported and tested. That's a
  structural change beyond "add tracking," and it's the reason this stopped being a bounded task.

  [1m1. Decorator at the composition root — recommended.[0m [38;5;153msrc/auth.js[39m exports a pure [38;5;153mlogin()[39m. [38;5;153msrc/tracking.js[39m exports
  [38;5;153mtrack()[39m. A third piece, [38;5;153mwithTracking(login)[39m, wraps one in the other, and [38;5;153mapp.js[39m calls the wrapped version. Buys you a
  [38;5;153mlogin()[39m with exactly one job — testable with no tracker stub at all — plus a tracker testable with no auth, and a
  single place where the two are joined, so no call site can forget. Costs: three modules and an indirection for a
  four-file project, and a stack trace that goes through a wrapper. Cheapest to undo — deleting the wrapper leaves both
  halves intact.

  [1m2. Tracking called inside [38;5;153mlogin()[39m.[0m [38;5;153mlogin()[39m imports [38;5;153mtrack()[39m and emits events itself. Buys you the simplest wiring and
  makes forgetting structurally impossible. Costs: [38;5;153mlogin()[39m now does authentication [3mand[0m telemetry, so every test of it
  needs the tracker stubbed, and the two concerns can't be changed independently. Hardest of the three to unpick later,
  because the coupling is inside the function everything calls.

  [1m3. Tracking called by the submit handler.[0m [38;5;153mlogin()[39m stays pure and returns its result; [38;5;153mapp.js[39m inspects it and calls
  [38;5;153mtrack()[39m. Buys you the fewest new concepts — no wrapper, no import inside auth — and keeps both halves pure. Costs: the
  wiring lives in the DOM layer, which is the one layer you can't unit-test without jsdom, and a second call site added
  later silently tracks nothing.

  Discarded: an event-bus / pub-sub design, where [38;5;153mlogin()[39m publishes and tracking subscribes. It's the textbook answer
  for decoupling telemetry, but it's real machinery — registry, lifecycle, ordering — for one producer and one consumer
  in a four-file repo, and it makes the flow harder to trace than any benefit it returns here.

